Description: antinomie: add a ptrace-free debug engine
 Let the host debug a program in the guest (gdb through the host's
 remote stub) without ptrace: no tracer pid, PTRACE_TRACEME still works,
 no SIGTRAP, nothing running in the guest. A sample that checks whether
 it is being debugged sees nothing.
 .
 Threads stop the way live process cores pause them: a task_work
 callback parks each thread in a killable sleep at its next return to
 user mode, with its registers frozen; a group that does not stop within
 3 s is reported, not claimed. Registers come from the kernel's regset
 view and travel as blobs tagged with their ELF note type, memory from
 access_remote_vm(). debug.c is the architecture-neutral engine and
 debug_x86.c knows the breakpoint instruction, trap recognition, single
 step, the debug registers and the register sets, so another architecture
 adds a file (built for that architecture only) and no wire change; the
 engine itself reads a stopped thread's PC with instruction_pointer().
 CAPS names the architecture (its ELF machine), and the host reads it
 when it identifies the guest, before it decodes any record.
 .
 What it offers: attach by identity, stop, continue and single-step;
 software breakpoints written only while the group runs and taken out at
 every stop (reads of the program's own memory through /proc/self/mem
 see the original bytes); hardware breakpoints and watchpoints through
 the kernel's breakpoint facility, invisible to a tracer; signal stops,
 where the debugger decides which signal a thread gets; a step that is
 exactly one user instruction, including a system call or a repeated
 string instruction; debug from the first instruction (EXEC flag DEBUG)
 and the auxiliary vector; following forks, vforks and execs with a
 session per child; non-stop threads with an event queue; and sessions
 that survive a reconnect or a restored snapshot and can be adopted
 again. A process core taken during a debug stop completes with the
 group still stopped, and STOP_RUN makes a session let its run go first.
 No step or request waits without bound.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - kernel/signal.c: get_signal() lets the engine stop a thread on a
   signal before it is delivered, signal_delivered() ends a step at a
   handler's first instruction, and complete_signal() queues a fatal
   signal of a debugged group so it is seen before the group dies.
 - kernel/entry/common.c: syscall_exit_work() ends a step at a system
   call's exit, before ptrace's own report.
 - kernel/fork.c (kernel_clone()) and fs/exec.c: fork, vfork and exec
   events, and debug registers for a new thread before it first runs.
 - mm/memory.c (__access_remote_vm()): reads of the program's memory
   through another process show the original bytes under a breakpoint.
 - kernel/antinomie/guest.c: the command table grows from 32 to 64
   entries, and the control worker polls every tick while a session
   exists.
 - kernel/antinomie/manual_core.c: a thread parked by the debugger runs
   a live core's work from its park.
 .
 Contract: the debug opcodes (0x1080 to 0x1092), DEBUG and the second
 capability word are additive within revision 17; an earlier guest
 answers them with -EOPNOTSUPP and an earlier host never asks for them.
